3. CONCEPT TEACHING
PART A — ARRAY
What is an array?

An array is a collection of elements stored under one variable name, usually in contiguous memory locations.

Example:

10   20   30   40   50

We can imagine:

Index:   0    1    2    3    4
         ↓    ↓    ↓    ↓    ↓
Array:  10   20   30   40   50

So:

array[0] = 10
array[1] = 20
array[2] = 30
Important

The first index is generally:

0

not 1.

Why is an array useful?

Suppose you need to store marks of 5 students.

Without an array:

mark1
mark2
mark3
mark4
mark5

With an array:

marks[5]

Much easier to manage.

Array memory

Suppose:

int A[5]

and each integer occupies 4 bytes.

If the starting address is:

1000

then conceptually:

Element	Address
A[0]	1000
A[1]	1004
A[2]	1008
A[3]	1012
A[4]	1016

Because elements are stored continuously.

Exam formula

For a zero-based array:

Address(A[i]) = Base Address + i × Size of each element

Example:

Base = 1000
i = 3
element size = 4 bytes

Address(A[3])
= 1000 + 3 × 4
= 1012
🚨 Exam trap

Don't use:

Base + (i + 1) × size

for a zero-based array.

PAPER-I LEVEL

Remember:

Array → contiguous memory + direct indexing

PAPER-II DEPTH

You should also understand why accessing an element by index is fast.

If I ask:

A[500]

the computer doesn't normally need to inspect:

A[0]
A[1]
A[2]
...
A[499]

It calculates the address directly.

Therefore:

Array access

O(1)

This is called constant time.

PART B — LINKED LIST

Now imagine that the elements don't need to be next to each other in memory.

Instead, each element contains:

DATA + ADDRESS OF NEXT ELEMENT

This is a linked list.

Example:

[10 | next] → [20 | next] → [30 | next] → NULL

Each box is called a node.

A node contains:

Data
+
Link/Pointer
Array vs Linked List
Array	Linked List
Contiguous memory	Nodes need not be contiguous
Direct indexing	No direct indexing
Fixed-size concept traditionally	Dynamic size
Insertion can require shifting	Insertion can be easier after locating position
Access by index: O(1)	Access by position: O(n)
Memory trick

ARRAY = ADJACENT

LINKED LIST = LINK

PART C — STACK

A stack works using:

LIFO

Last In, First Out

Think of a stack of plates.

       ┌─────┐
       │ 30  │ ← TOP
       ├─────┤
       │ 20  │
       ├─────┤
       │ 10  │
       └─────┘

If you put 40:

       40 ← TOP
       30
       20
       10

If you remove an element, 40 comes out first.

Stack operations
PUSH

Insert an element.

push(40)
POP

Remove the top element.

pop()
PEEK / TOP

Look at the top element without removing it.

Example

Initially:

10
20
30

Push 40:

10
20
30
40 ← TOP

Pop:

40 removed

Remaining:

10
20
30 ← TOP
Memory trick

STACK = LIFO

Think:

Last plate placed → first plate removed

PART D — QUEUE

Queue works using:

FIFO

First In, First Out

Think about people standing in a line.

FRONT
 ↓
10 → 20 → 30 → 40
                    ↑
                  REAR

The person who entered first leaves first.

Queue operations
ENQUEUE

Insert at the rear.

DEQUEUE

Remove from the front.

Example:

10 → 20 → 30

Enqueue 40:

10 → 20 → 30 → 40

Dequeue:

20 → 30 → 40

10 was the first element, so 10 leaves first.

Stack vs Queue
Stack	Queue
LIFO	FIFO
Last in → first out	First in → first out
Insert/remove at top	Insert at rear, remove at front
PUSH	ENQUEUE
POP	DEQUEUE
Very important memory trick

STACK → LIFO

QUEUE → FIFO

4. SEARCHING

Suppose we have:

10 20 30 40 50

and want to find:

40

There are different searching techniques.

Today we'll learn two fundamental ones.

Linear Search

Linear search checks elements one by one.

Array:

10  20  30  40  50
↑

Is it 40?

No.

10  20  30  40  50
    ↑

Is it 40?

No.

10  20  30  40  50
        ↑

No.

10  20  30  40  50
            ↑

Yes!

Found.

Linear Search complexity
Best case

The element is first:

[40, 10, 20, 30, 50]
 ↑

Only one comparison.

Best case:

O(1)

Worst case

Element is at the end:

10 20 30 40 50
            ↑

We check almost everything.

Worst case:

O(n)

Average case

Approximately:

O(n)

Binary Search

Binary search is much faster, but there is an important condition:

The data must be sorted.

Example:

10 20 30 40 50 60 70

Search:

60

Instead of checking from the beginning, look at the middle.

Middle:

40

60 is greater than 40.

Therefore:

Ignore the left half.

Remaining:

50 60 70

Middle:

60

Found.

Why is binary search fast?

Every step approximately cuts the search space in half.

For example:

1000 elements
↓
500
↓
250
↓
125
↓
62
↓
31
↓
...

Therefore its time complexity is:

Worst case:

O(log n)

Linear vs Binary Search
Feature	Linear Search	Binary Search
Data needs sorting?	No	Yes
Approach	One by one	Divide search space
Best case	O(1)	O(1)
Worst case	O(n)	O(log n)
Simple?	Very simple	More complex
Key requirement	None	Sorted data
🚨 Major SSC trap

If the question says:

Binary search is applied to an array.

Immediately ask yourself:

Is the array sorted?

If not, standard binary search cannot be directly applied.

5. BIG-O

This is extremely important for CSE.

Big-O describes how the running time or resource requirement of an algorithm grows with input size.

Suppose:

n = number of elements

An algorithm that does approximately one operation per element:

n operations

has:

O(n)

Common complexities

From generally smaller growth to larger growth:

O(1)
O(log n)
O(n)
O(n log n)
O(n²)
O(2ⁿ)
O(n!)

You should memorize this order.

Memory trick

Constant → Log → Linear → N-log-N → Square → Exponential → Factorial

